{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB05316 - MONDO_18466",
  "verbalization": "Pimavanserin is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hereditary Late Onset Parkinson Disease (HLOPD) through a series of indirect pathways involving atypical juvenile Parkinsonism and cycrimine. The evidence suggests that Pimavanserin may act as a mediator in the progression of HLOPD, as it is associated with both atypical juvenile Parkinsonism and early-onset Parkinsonism-intellectual disability syndrome, which are both linked to HLOPD. These conditions are indicated to be mediated by cycrimine, which in turn is linked to HLOPD. The overlapping pathways suggest that Pimavanserin may influence the disease process through shared mechanisms, potentially by modulating neurotransmitter systems or cellular pathways involved in Parkinson’s disease. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Pimavanserin in the broader context of neurodegenerative disorders, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
